\title{Polarisation Cancellation in Exponential Product Gates:\\
the Sharp Constant, the Corner Theorem,\\ and an Affine-Readout Barrier}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Networks whose nonlinearity is the exponential $u\mapsto e^{u}$ synthesise
multiplication by \emph{polarisation}: from a soft squaring unit
$\Sh(u)=\bigl(e^{hu}+e^{-hu}-2\bigr)/h^{2}
      = u^{2}+h^{2}u^{4}/12+h^{4}u^{6}/360+\cdots$
one forms the width-four product gate
$\Ph(x,y)=\tfrac14\bigl(\Sh(x+y)-\Sh(x-y)\bigr)$, which approximates $xy$ to
$O(h^{2})$. We determine the error of this gate exactly.

Writing $\gamma(t)=e^{t}+e^{-t}-2-t^{2}$ for the remainder of $2\cosh$ after its
quadratic Taylor polynomial, we prove the exact identity
\[
  \Ph(x,y)-xy=\frac{\gamma\bigl(h(x+y)\bigr)-\gamma\bigl(h|x-y|\bigr)}{4h^{2}},
\]
which exhibits the error as the \emph{increment} of a single even, monotone
function. From it we derive: (i) a two-sided polarised bound with the correct
shape $(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}$ rather than the sum $(x+y)^{4}+(x-y)^{4}$ produced by
branchwise estimation; (ii) the sharp leading term $h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})/6$ with
explicit remainder $h^{4}/21$; and (iii) a closed form for the supremum, valid
for every $h>0$ with no Taylor expansion,
\[
  \sup_{[0,1]^{2}}\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|
   =\frac{e^{2h}+e^{-2h}-2-4h^{2}}{4h^{2}}
   =\frac{h^{2}}{3}+\frac{2h^{4}}{45}+\cdots,
\]
attained at the corner $(1,1)$. We then show the $\Theta(h^{2})$ rate is
intrinsic: no scalar gain, and no affine read-out
$\lambda \Ph(x,y)+\mu \Sh(x)+\nu \Sh(y)+\kappa$ reusing the gate's own squaring
units, achieves $o(h^{2})$; the obstruction is exhibited by a mixed second
difference that annihilates $\mu,\nu,\kappa$ exactly. Finally we abstract the
mechanism: for every generator $g(t)=t^{2}+\gamma_{g}(t)$ whose remainder is
even, vanishes at $0$ and is non-decreasing on $[0,\infty)$, the maximal error of
the polarisation gate over $[0,1]^{2}$ equals $\gamma_{g}(2h)/(4h^{2})$ and is
attained at $(1,1)$; if $\gamma_{g}(t)=ct^{4}+O(t^{6})$ this is
$4ch^{2}+O(h^{4})$. The exponential constant $1/3$ is simply $4c$ with $c=1/12$.
A non-monotone counterexample, $g(t)=t^{2}-t^{4}$, shows the monotonicity
hypothesis is load-bearing. Applications: quadratic forms on $[0,1]^{n}$ with
error $(h^{2}/3+h^{4}/21)\lVert A\rVert_{1}$, and a chained two-gate bound
$\tfrac34 h^{2}$ demonstrating additive rather than compounding accumulation.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Polarisation identity; exponential activation;
product gate; sharp approximation constant; monotone remainder; mixed second
difference; approximation barrier.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Multiplication is not a layer operation}

A feed-forward layer computes $x\mapsto\sigma(Wx+b)$: an affine map followed by a
pointwise univariate nonlinearity. Such a map cannot compute the bilinear form
$(x,y)\mapsto xy$ in one step, whatever $\sigma$ may be, because $\sigma$ never
sees two coordinates at once. Yet products dominate the functional vocabulary of
physics, statistics and modern architectures --- energies, forces, moments,
covariances, attention scores, polynomial features.

The classical remedy is the \emph{polarisation identity}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pol}
  xy=\frac{(x+y)^{2}-(x-y)^{2}}{4},
\end{equation}
which reduces multiplication to squaring, a univariate operation. If
$\sigma(u)=u^{2}$ the reduction is exact. For other activations one first
synthesises an approximate square, then polarises.

\subsection{Exponential networks and the soft square}

Fix a scale $h>0$ and define the \emph{soft squaring unit}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:soft}
  \Sh(u)=\frac{e^{hu}+e^{-hu}-2}{h^{2}}.
\end{equation}
Two exponential units and a linear read-out. Since
$e^{hu}+e^{-hu}=2+h^{2}u^{2}+h^{4}u^{4}/12+h^{6}u^{6}/360+\cdots$, we have
$\Sh(u)=u^{2}+h^{2}u^{4}/12+h^{4}u^{6}/360+\cdots$, so $\Sh\to(\cdot)^{2}$
locally uniformly as $h\to0$. Substituting into \eqref{eq:pol} gives the
\emph{product gate}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gate}
  \Ph(x,y)=\frac{\Sh(x+y)-\Sh(x-y)}{4},
\end{equation}
of width $4$ and depth $1$.

\subsection{The question, and why the obvious answer is wrong}

How large is $\Ph(x,y)-xy$ on $[0,1]^{2}$? The branchwise estimate is immediate:
$|\Sh(u)-u^{2}|\lesssim h^{2}u^{4}/12$, so by the triangle inequality
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sum}
  \bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|\lesssim
  \frac{h^{2}\bigl[(x+y)^{4}+(x-y)^{4}\bigr]}{24}.
\end{equation}
This is true but structurally wrong. Set $y=0$: since $\Sh$ is even,
$\Ph(x,0)=\tfrac14(\Sh(x)-\Sh(-x))=0$ \emph{exactly}, while \eqref{eq:sum}
predicts $h^{2}x^{4}/12>0$. The estimate cannot see that the two polarisation
branches make correlated errors that cancel.

The conjecture settled here is that the correct shape is the \emph{difference} of
fourth powers, whence
$\sup_{[0,1]^{2}}|\Ph-xy| = h^{2}/3+O(h^{4})$, attained at $(1,1)$.

\subsection{Results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exact error identity} (Theorem~\ref{thm:identity}).
\item \textbf{Two-sided polarised bound} (Theorem~\ref{thm:polarised}):
  $\dfrac{h^{2}[(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}]}{48}\le \Ph(x,y)-xy\le
   \dfrac{h^{2}[(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}]}{24}$; the error is non-negative and
   vanishes identically on the axes.
\item \textbf{Sharp local constant} (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}):
  $\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy-h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})/6\bigr|\le h^{4}/21$. Since
  $(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}=8xy(x^{2}+y^{2})$, the sharp constant is $/48$, not
  $/24$.
\item \textbf{Corner theorem and closed form}
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:corner-max}, \ref{thm:closed}): for every $h>0$ the
  supremum equals $\bigl(e^{2h}+e^{-2h}-2-4h^{2}\bigr)/(4h^{2})$ and is attained
  at $(1,1)$. The $O(h^{4})$ is removable.
\item \textbf{No scalar debiasing} (Theorem~\ref{thm:scalar}) and
  \textbf{no affine debiasing} (Theorem~\ref{thm:affine}).
\item \textbf{Universality} (Theorems~\ref{thm:univ}, \ref{thm:design},
  \ref{thm:quartic}) and necessity of monotonicity
  (Proposition~\ref{prop:neg}).
\item \textbf{Applications}: quadratic forms (Theorem~\ref{thm:quadform}) and
  chained gates (Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The methodological point}

The lossy step in \eqref{eq:sum} is $|E_{+}-E_{-}|\le|E_{+}|+|E_{-}|$, which is
lossless exactly when $E_{-}=0$ and therefore \emph{cannot detect cancellation}.
The replacement is a \emph{monotonicity certificate}: cancellation between
polarisation branches is a statement about how the remainder \emph{changes}, not
about how \emph{large} it is.

\section{Notation}

Throughout $h>0$ and $I=[0,1]$.

\begin{definition}[Soft squaring unit]
$\Sh(u)=\bigl(e^{hu}+e^{-hu}-2\bigr)/h^{2}$ for $h\neq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Product gate]
$\Ph(x,y)=\tfrac14\bigl(\Sh(x+y)-\Sh(x-y)\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cosh remainder]\label{def:gamma}
$\gamma(t)=e^{t}+e^{-t}-2-t^{2}$.
\end{definition}

Elementary properties: $\gamma$ is even, $\gamma(0)=0$, $\gamma(|t|)=\gamma(t)$,
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:series}
  \gamma(t)=\sum_{k\ge2}\frac{2t^{2k}}{(2k)!}
   =\frac{t^{4}}{12}+\frac{t^{6}}{360}+\frac{t^{8}}{20160}+\cdots
\end{equation}
has non-negative coefficients. Also $\Sh(u)=u^{2}+\gamma(hu)/h^{2}$.

\begin{definition}[Error set]
$\Ecal(h)=\bigl\{\,|\Ph(x,y)-xy| : (x,y)\in I\times I\,\bigr\}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The exact error identity}

\begin{theorem}[Exact error identity]\label{thm:identity}
For $h\neq0$ and all $x,y\in\R$,
\[
  \Ph(x,y)-xy=\frac{\gamma\bigl(h(x+y)\bigr)-\gamma\bigl(h(x-y)\bigr)}{4h^{2}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Sh(u)=u^{2}+\gamma(hu)/h^{2}$, hence
$\Ph(x,y)=\tfrac14\bigl[(x+y)^{2}-(x-y)^{2}\bigr]
 +\bigl[\gamma(h(x+y))-\gamma(h(x-y))\bigr]/(4h^{2})$, and the first term is
$xy$ by \eqref{eq:pol}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Normalised form]\label{cor:norm}
For $h>0$,
$\Ph(x,y)-xy=\bigl[\gamma(h(x+y))-\gamma(h|x-y|)\bigr]/(4h^{2})$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Exactness on the axes]\label{cor:axes}
$\Ph(x,0)=\Ph(0,y)=0$ for all $x,y$ and all $h\neq0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[One-sidedness]\label{cor:onesided}
For $h>0$ and $x,y\ge0$, $\Ph(x,y)-xy\ge0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\gamma$ is non-decreasing on $[0,\infty)$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}) and
$0\le h|x-y|\le h(x+y)$, so the numerator in Corollary~\ref{cor:norm} is
non-negative.
\end{proof}

The gate systematically \emph{overshoots} on the positive quadrant; this
asymmetry drives the accumulation results of Section~\ref{sec:apps}.

\section{Cancellation via monotonicity}

\begin{lemma}[Cubic bracket for $2\sinh$]\label{lem:cubic}
For $t\in[0,1]$: $\;t^{3}/3\le e^{t}-e^{-t}-2t\le t^{3}/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both bounds follow from truncated exponential series with explicit remainder
control: for the lower bound compare $e^{t}$ with its degree-$6$ Taylor
polynomial from below and $e^{-t}$ with the same polynomial from above, and use
$t\le1$ to absorb the degree-$7$ tail; the upper bound is the same computation
truncated at degree $4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[$\gamma$ is monotone]\label{lem:mono}
$\gamma'(t)=e^{t}-e^{-t}-2t=2(\sinh t-t)\ge0$ for $t\ge0$; hence $\gamma$ is
non-decreasing on $[0,\infty)$ and $\gamma\ge0$ everywhere.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Slack monotonicity]\label{lem:slack}
The slack functions $U(t)=t^{4}/6-\gamma(t)$ and $L(t)=\gamma(t)-t^{4}/12$ are
non-decreasing on $[0,1]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$U'(t)=\tfrac23 t^{3}-(e^{t}-e^{-t}-2t)\ge\tfrac23t^{3}-\tfrac12t^{3}>0$ and
$L'(t)=(e^{t}-e^{-t}-2t)-\tfrac13t^{3}\ge0$, by Lemma~\ref{lem:cubic}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cancellation estimates]\label{lem:cancel}
For $0\le b\le a\le1$:
$\dfrac{a^{4}-b^{4}}{12}\le\gamma(a)-\gamma(b)\le\dfrac{a^{4}-b^{4}}{6}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$U(b)\le U(a)$ and $L(b)\le L(a)$, rearranged.
\end{proof}

Both inequalities are equalities at $a=b$, so no spurious error appears on the
diagonal.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided polarised bound]\label{thm:polarised}
For $0<h\le\tfrac12$ and $x,y\in[0,1]$,
\[
  \frac{h^{2}\bigl[(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}\bigr]}{48}
   \le \Ph(x,y)-xy \le
  \frac{h^{2}\bigl[(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}\bigr]}{24}.
\]
In particular $|\Ph(x,y)-xy|\le h^{2}[(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}]/24$, which vanishes
identically on the axes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $a=h(x+y)$, $b=h|x-y|$. Then $0\le b\le a\le 2h\le1$ since $|x-y|\le x+y$
for $x,y\ge0$. Lemma~\ref{lem:cancel} applies; moreover $a^{4}=h^{4}(x+y)^{4}$
and $b^{4}=h^{4}(x-y)^{4}$. Substituting into Corollary~\ref{cor:norm} and
dividing by $4h^{2}$ gives the two displayed constants. Non-negativity
(Corollary~\ref{cor:onesided}) converts the upper bound into one on the absolute
value.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why branchwise estimation must fail]\label{rem:fail}
Suppose one bounds branches separately, $\gamma(a)\le\alpha a^{4}$ and
$\gamma(b)\ge\beta b^{4}$ with $\alpha>\beta$ (necessarily, since
$\gamma(t)/t^{4}$ is not constant). At $a=b$ the resulting bound reads
$(\alpha-\beta)a^{4}>0$ while the truth is $0$. Since $a=b$ corresponds to the
diagonal $x=y$, no separate-branch scheme is sharp there.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[Quartic approximation of $\gamma$]\label{lem:quartic}
For $|t|\le1$: $\bigl|\gamma(t)-t^{4}/12-t^{6}/360\bigr|\le t^{8}/17920$; hence
$\bigl|\gamma(t)-t^{4}/12\bigr|\le t^{6}/350$ and, one-sidedly,
$\gamma(t)\le t^{4}/12+t^{6}/352$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp asymptotics]\label{thm:sharp}
For $0<h\le\tfrac12$ and $x,y\in[0,1]$,
\[
  \left|\,\Ph(x,y)-xy-\frac{h^{2}\,xy\,(x^{2}+y^{2})}{6}\,\right|
   \le \frac{h^{4}}{21}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $a=h(x+y)$, $b=h|x-y|$ (so $|a|,|b|\le1$), Lemma~\ref{lem:quartic} gives
$\bigl|(\gamma(a)-\gamma(b))-(a^{4}-b^{4})/12\bigr|\le(a^{6}+b^{6})/350
 \le 65h^{6}/350$ using $(x+y)^{6}\le64$, $(x-y)^{6}\le1$. Dividing by $4h^{2}$
and noting $(a^{4}-b^{4})/(48h^{2})=h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})/6$ --- because
$(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)^{4}=8xy(x^{2}+y^{2})$ --- yields $65h^{4}/1400\le h^{4}/21$.
\end{proof}

Thus within the sandwich of Theorem~\ref{thm:polarised} it is the \emph{lower}
constant that is attained: the conjectured $/24$ is right in shape but a factor
$2$ conservative.

\begin{corollary}[Uniform bound]\label{cor:unif}
For $0<h\le\tfrac12$ and $x,y\in[0,1]$: $|\Ph(x,y)-xy|\le h^{2}/3+h^{4}/21$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$xy(x^{2}+y^{2})\le2$ on $[0,1]^{2}$; add the remainder of
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\end{proof}

\section{The corner theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Corner dominance]\label{thm:corner}
For every $h>0$ and every $(x,y)\in[0,1]^{2}$,
$\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|\le\gamma(2h)/(4h^{2})=\Ph(1,1)-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:onesided} the error is non-negative, hence equals
$\bigl[\gamma(h(x+y))-\gamma(h|x-y|)\bigr]/(4h^{2})$. Now $\gamma$ is
non-decreasing on $[0,\infty)$ and $0\le h(x+y)\le2h$, so
$\gamma(h(x+y))\le\gamma(2h)$, while $\gamma(h|x-y|)\ge0$. At $x=y=1$ the
arguments are $2h$ and $0$, and $\gamma(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The maximum is attained at $(1,1)$]\label{thm:corner-max}
For every $h>0$, $\gamma(2h)/(4h^{2})$ is the greatest element of $\Ecal(h)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For every $h>0$,
\[
  \sup_{(x,y)\in[0,1]^{2}}\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|
   =\frac{e^{2h}+e^{-2h}-2-4h^{2}}{4h^{2}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Expansion]\label{cor:expansion}
By \eqref{eq:series} with $t=2h$,
\[
  \sup_{[0,1]^{2}}\bigl|\Ph-xy\bigr|
   =\frac{h^{2}}{3}+\frac{2h^{4}}{45}+\frac{h^{6}}{315}+\cdots ,
\]
so $\bigl|\sup\Ecal(h)-h^{2}/3\bigr|\le h^{4}/21$ for $0<h\le\tfrac12$. The true
remainder coefficient is $2/45\approx0.0444$ against the certified
$1/21\approx0.0476$.
\end{corollary}

Two comments. First, the $O(h^{4})$ of the original conjecture is
\emph{removable}: the supremum has a closed form for every $h>0$. Second, the
proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:corner} uses \emph{no Taylor expansion}: only
$\gamma\ge0$, $\gamma(0)=0$ and monotonicity --- that is, only $\sinh t\ge t$.

\section{Barriers: the $\Theta(h^{2})$ rate is intrinsic}

\subsection{No scalar debiasing}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} the leading error is $h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})/6$, which
is \emph{not} proportional to $xy$.

\begin{lemma}[Two probes]\label{lem:probes}
For $0<h\le\tfrac12$: $\bigl|\Ph(1,1)-1-h^{2}/3\bigr|\le h^{4}/21$ and
$\bigl|\Ph(1,\tfrac12)-\tfrac12-5h^{2}/48\bigr|\le h^{4}/21$.
\end{lemma}

The relative leading errors are $h^{2}/3$ and $5h^{2}/24$: they differ.

\begin{theorem}[No scalar debiasing]\label{thm:scalar}
For every $h\in(0,\tfrac12]$ and every $\lambda\in\R$ (possibly depending on
$h$),
\[
  \max\Bigl\{\bigl|\lambda \Ph(1,1)-1\bigr|,\;
             \bigl|\lambda \Ph(1,\tfrac12)-\tfrac12\bigr|\Bigr\}
   \ \ge\ \frac{h^{2}}{100}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If both were $<h^{2}/100$, then $\lambda\approx1-h^{2}/3$ from the first probe
and $\lambda\approx1-5h^{2}/24$ from the second; these differ by
$h^{2}/8+O(h^{4})$, exceeding the slack $2h^{2}/100$ for $h\le\tfrac12$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{No affine debiasing}

Consider the affine read-out
\begin{equation}\label{eq:affine}
  N_{\lambda\mu\nu\kappa}(x,y)
   =\lambda\,\Ph(x,y)+\mu\,\Sh(x)+\nu\,\Sh(y)+\kappa,
\end{equation}
with error $E(x,y)=N_{\lambda\mu\nu\kappa}(x,y)-xy$. This costs nothing new:
$\Sh(x)$ and $\Sh(y)$ are computable from the same four exponential units.

\begin{definition}[Mixed second difference]
For $F:\R^{2}\to\R$ and $a,d$ (first coordinate), $b,c$ (second),
$D[F]=F(a,b)-F(a,c)-F(d,b)+F(d,c)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Annihilation]\label{lem:annihilate}
$D$ annihilates every $F(x,y)=\varphi(x)+\psi(y)+\kappa$.
\end{lemma}

This is exact --- no estimates. Applying $D$ to $E$ eliminates $\mu,\nu,\kappa$
completely. Take $R_{1}$ with corners $(1,1),(1,0),(0,1),(0,0)$ and $R_{2}$ with
corners $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12),(\tfrac12,0),(0,\tfrac12),(0,0)$; on both, three of
the four vertices lie on an axis, where $\Ph=0$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:axes}) and
$\Sh(0)=0$.

\begin{lemma}[The two scalar equations]\label{lem:two-eq}
For $h\neq0$: $\;D_{R_{1}}[E]=\lambda \Ph(1,1)-1$ and
$D_{R_{2}}[E]=\lambda \Ph(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)-\tfrac14$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[No affine debiasing]\label{thm:affine}
For every $h\in(0,\tfrac12]$ and all $\lambda,\mu,\nu,\kappa\in\R$ there exists
$(x,y)\in[0,1]^{2}$ with
$\bigl|N_{\lambda\mu\nu\kappa}(x,y)-xy\bigr|\ge h^{2}/210$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If not, $|E|<h^{2}/210$ at each of the seven probe points
$(1,1),(1,0),(0,1),(0,0),(\tfrac12,\tfrac12),(\tfrac12,0),(0,\tfrac12)$, so by
Lemma~\ref{lem:two-eq} and the triangle inequality, with $A=\Ph(1,1)$ and
$B=\Ph(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$,
\[
 |\lambda A-1|<\tfrac{4h^{2}}{210},\qquad
 |\lambda B-\tfrac14|<\tfrac{4h^{2}}{210}.
\]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, $A=1+h^{2}/3+O(h^{4})$ and
$B=\tfrac14+h^{2}/48+O(h^{4})$, so $A-4B=h^{2}/4+O(h^{4})$. Subtracting four
times the second bound from the first gives $|\lambda|\,h^{2}/4\lesssim
2h^{2}/21$, hence $|\lambda|<\tfrac12$; but then
$|\lambda A-1|\ge1-\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac32=\tfrac14$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Not fourth order]\label{cor:notfourth}
For every constant $C$ there is $h\in(0,\tfrac12]$ such that for all
$\lambda,\mu,\nu,\kappa$ the error of $N_{\lambda\mu\nu\kappa}$ exceeds $Ch^{4}$
somewhere on $[0,1]^{2}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Pick $n\in\N$ with $n>210C$ and $h=1/(n+2)$; then $Ch^{4}<h^{2}/210$ and
Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} applies.
\end{proof}

Define the \emph{correction rank} of the gate as the minimum number of
additional nonlinear units needed to reach $o(h^{2})$. Theorem~\ref{thm:affine}
says it is at least $1$: two probes with different ratios of quartic error to
bilinear value cannot be reconciled by a scalar correction, and separable
corrections are invisible to the mixed difference.

\section{Universality}

\begin{definition}[Even generator]\label{def:evengen}
An \emph{even generator} is $g(t)=t^{2}+\gamma_{g}(t)$ where the remainder
$\gamma_{g}$ satisfies (i) $\gamma_{g}(-t)=\gamma_{g}(t)$; (ii)
$\gamma_{g}(0)=0$; (iii) $\gamma_{g}$ is non-decreasing on $[0,\infty)$.
\end{definition}

Any power series $\sum_{k\ge2}c_{2k}t^{2k}$ with $c_{2k}\ge0$ qualifies, as does
$|t|^{p}$ for $p>0$. Set
$G_{h}(x,y)=\bigl[g(h(x+y))-g(h(x-y))\bigr]/(4h^{2})$; as in
Theorem~\ref{thm:identity},
$G_{h}(x,y)-xy=\bigl[\gamma_{g}(h(x+y))-\gamma_{g}(h|x-y|)\bigr]/(4h^{2})$.

\begin{theorem}[Universal corner theorem]\label{thm:univ}
For every even generator $g$ and every $h>0$, the maximum of
$|G_{h}(x,y)-xy|$ over $[0,1]^{2}$ is attained at $(1,1)$ and equals
$\gamma_{g}(2h)/(4h^{2})=\bigl(g(2h)-4h^{2}\bigr)/(4h^{2})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Design criterion]\label{thm:design}
If $\gamma_{g_{1}}\le\gamma_{g_{2}}$ pointwise, then for all $h>0$ the maximal
error of the first gate is at most that of the second.
\end{theorem}

There is therefore \emph{no trade-off}: minimising the remainder pointwise
minimises the worst-case error, uniformly in $h$.

\begin{theorem}[Universal quartic constant]\label{thm:quartic}
If $|\gamma_{g}(t)-c\,t^{4}|\le Ct^{6}$ for $|t|\le1$, then for $0<h\le\tfrac12$,
\[
 \Bigl|\max_{[0,1]^{2}}|G_{h}-xy|-4c\,h^{2}\Bigr|\le 16C\,h^{4}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\gamma_{g}(2h)/(4h^{2})-4ch^{2}=\bigl[\gamma_{g}(2h)-c(2h)^{4}\bigr]/(4h^{2})$,
of absolute value at most $C(2h)^{6}/(4h^{2})=16Ch^{4}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The exponential instance]\label{cor:expinstance}
For $g(t)=e^{t}+e^{-t}-2$ we have $\gamma_{g}=\gamma$, $c=1/12$, $C=1/352$, so
$\bigl|\max_{[0,1]^{2}}|\Ph-xy|-h^{2}/3\bigr|\le h^{4}/22$. The constant $1/3$ is
exactly $4c$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Pure quartic generators]\label{prop:quartic}
For $g(t)=t^{2}+c\,t^{4}$ with $c\ge0$ the maximum is $4ch^{2}$ \emph{exactly},
for all $h>0$. Hence the $O(h^{4})$ in the exponential case originates entirely
in the sextic and higher Taylor coefficients of $2\cosh$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Degeneration and comparison]\label{prop:degen}
At $c=0$, $g(t)=t^{2}$ and the gate is exact. Moreover for $0<h\le\tfrac12$ the
exponential gate is never better than the pure quartic gate with the same
leading coefficient: $\tfrac13h^{2}\le\max_{[0,1]^{2}}|\Ph-xy|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity is load-bearing]\label{prop:neg}
For the non-monotone generator $g(t)=t^{2}-t^{4}$, the closed form of
Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} evaluates to $-4h^{2}<0$, which cannot be the maximum of
an absolute value. Indeed $G_{h}(x,y)-xy=-2h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})$ exactly, so
this gate \emph{undershoots} and the true maximum of $|G_{h}-xy|$ is $+4h^{2}$,
attained at $(1,1)$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Applications}\label{sec:apps}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp quadratic-form error]\label{thm:quadform}
Let $A\in\R^{n\times n}$, $x\in[0,1]^{n}$, $0<h\le\tfrac12$. Then
\[
 \Bigl|\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}\Ph(x_{i},x_{j})-\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}x_{i}x_{j}\Bigr|
  \le\Bigl(\frac{h^{2}}{3}+\frac{h^{4}}{21}\Bigr)\sum_{i,j}|A_{ij}|.
\]
\end{theorem}

A quadratic form on $[0,1]^{n}$ is thus computed by a single layer of $4n^{2}$
exponential units to accuracy $(h^{2}/3+h^{4}/21)\lVert A\rVert_{1}$: a
threefold improvement on the branchwise constant $h^{2}\lVert A\rVert_{1}$,
uniform in $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Box bound]\label{thm:box}
Let $M>0$ with $2Mh\le1$ and $0\le x,y\le M$. Then
$\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|\le M^{4}h^{2}/3+M^{6}h^{4}/22$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity of $\gamma$ bounds the error by $\gamma(2Mh)/(4h^{2})$; apply
$\gamma(u)\le u^{4}/12+u^{6}/352$ at $u=2Mh$ and simplify.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Output range]\label{lem:range}
For $0<h\le\tfrac14$ and $x,y\in[0,1]$: $0\le \Ph(x,y)\le\tfrac{33}{32}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Two chained gates]\label{thm:tree}
For $0<h\le\tfrac14$ and $x,y,z\in[0,1]$,
\[
  \bigl|\Ph\bigl(\Ph(x,y),z\bigr)-xyz\bigr|\ \le\ \frac{3h^{2}}{4}
  \ =\ \frac94\cdot\frac{h^{2}}{3}.
\]
Moreover the left-hand expression inside the absolute value is non-negative.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $Q=\Ph(x,y)\in[0,\tfrac{33}{32}]$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:range}), split
$\Ph(Q,z)-xyz=[\Ph(Q,z)-Qz]+[(Q-xy)z]$. Bound the first by
Theorem~\ref{thm:box} with $M=\tfrac{33}{32}$ and the second by
$h^{2}/3+h^{4}/22$; use $h^{4}\le h^{2}/16$. Non-negativity is
Corollary~\ref{cor:onesided} applied to each bracket.
\end{proof}

The additive prediction is $2\cdot h^{2}/3\approx0.667h^{2}$; the certified
constant $0.75h^{2}$ is within $13\%$. A multiplicative model would predict a
factor growing like $(33/32)^{4d}$ in the gate count $d$, which does not occur:
the excess over additivity is paid once, not once per level. Since all gate
errors share the same sign, they cannot conspire adversarially.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Exact worst-case error]
Given $h>0$, return $\bigl(e^{2h}+e^{-2h}-2-4h^{2}\bigr)/(4h^{2})$ with
maximiser $(1,1)$; cost $O(1)$. For $h\lesssim10^{-4}$ the expression suffers
catastrophic cancellation in double precision; use instead the series
$h^{2}/3+2h^{4}/45+h^{6}/315+\cdots$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Scale selection]
Given $\varepsilon>0$, find the largest $h$ with
$\gamma(2h)/(4h^{2})\le\varepsilon$. The map
$h\mapsto\sum_{k\ge2}2^{2k-1}h^{2k-2}/(2k)!$ is strictly increasing, so
bisection converges monotonically; the leading-order answer is
$h\approx\sqrt{3\varepsilon}$ and the exact answer is slightly smaller.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Barrier certificate]
Given $h$ and $(\lambda,\mu,\nu,\kappa)$, evaluate the error of \eqref{eq:affine}
at the seven probe points and return the maximum. By Theorem~\ref{thm:affine}
this maximum is at least $h^{2}/210$, so the routine is a complete certificate:
no search over the square is required.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the proof needed}

Three ingredients: the exact identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:identity}), which
reduces a bivariate problem to a univariate increment; monotonicity of the
remainder (Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}), equivalent to $\sinh t\ge t$, which alone gives
the corner theorem, the closed form, one-sidedness, the box bound and the whole
universality theory; and slack monotonicity (Lemma~\ref{lem:slack}), which gives
the two-sided bound with matching shape. Taylor analysis is confined to the
quantitative statements.

\subsection{The general lesson}

Whenever a bilinear quantity is synthesised from a univariate approximant by
polarisation, the error is a \emph{difference} of remainders. Estimating it as a
sum is lossy not by a constant but \emph{in shape}, producing bounds that fail
to vanish where the true error vanishes identically. The remedy is structural:
identify the remainder as a monotone function and estimate its increment. The
same argument applies verbatim to any $[\Phi(u+v)-\Phi(u-v)]/4$ with $\Phi$ an
even approximant of $(\cdot)^{2}$, and with sign changes to second-difference
stencils.

\subsection{Limits}

On a signed domain $[-1,1]^{2}$ the error is negative where $xy<0$ and the
maximum of $|\cdot|$ is attained at all four corners with the same magnitude
$\gamma(2h)/(4h^{2})$; one-sidedness, and hence the accumulation argument, is
genuinely restricted to the positive quadrant. The affine barrier constant
$h^{2}/210$ is not optimal; numerical minimisation of the worst probe error
indicates the true optimum is close to $h^{2}/32$.

\section{Future work}

\textbf{Higher-order gates.} A Richardson-type combination such as
$\tfrac43 \Sh(u)-\tfrac13 S_{2h}(u)$ has remainder starting at order $t^{6}$,
predicting $O(h^{4})$ error at width $8$. The barrier of
Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} shows this cost is unavoidable at width $4$, pinning
the width--order trade-off from both sides; the exact exchange rate is open.

\textbf{Correction rank.} Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} lower-bounds the correction
rank by $1$. Determining it exactly, and generalising the mixed-difference
obstruction to rank-$r$ corrections via higher finite differences on richer
probe grids, is the natural next step.

\textbf{Deep trees.} The mechanism (box bound, output range, one-sidedness) is
iterable; the conjecture is that a depth-$d$ tree with $d$ gates errs by at most
$(d/3+o(1))h^{2}$. The obstacle is controlling the operand bound
$M_{k+1}=M_{k}^{2}+M_{k}^{4}h^{2}/3+\cdots$.

\textbf{Signed and unbounded domains.} Extending to $[-M,M]^{2}$ and to
sub-Gaussian inputs, where $\E\,\gamma(h(X+Y))$ replaces a supremum, would give
statistical rather than worst-case guarantees.

\textbf{Beyond the exponential.} Non-analytic generators $t^{2}+|t|^{p}$,
$2<p<4$, give error $\Theta(h^{p-2})$, showing that smoothness buys the rate;
generators with negative remainders undershoot, and deliberately alternating
signs across a tree could yield accumulated bias $o(dh^{2})$.

\textbf{Minimax activations.} Theorem~\ref{thm:design} makes pointwise remainder
comparison a total order on gate quality. Combined with realisability
constraints, this poses a clean minimax problem: minimise $\gamma_{g}(2h)$ over
the realisable set.

\section{Conclusion}

The error of the width-four exponential product gate on $[0,1]^{2}$ is known
exactly:
\[
 \max_{[0,1]^{2}}\bigl|\Ph(x,y)-xy\bigr|
  =\frac{e^{2h}+e^{-2h}-2-4h^{2}}{4h^{2}}
  =\frac{h^{2}}{3}+\frac{2h^{4}}{45}+\cdots,
\]
attained at $(1,1)$, with sharp pointwise leading term
$h^{2}xy(x^{2}+y^{2})/6$. The rate $\Theta(h^{2})$ survives every scalar and
every affine read-out. All of this follows from one structural fact --- the
error is the increment of an even monotone remainder between $h|x-y|$ and
$h(x+y)$ --- which also yields a complete theory of polarisation gates for
arbitrary even monotone generators, in which the constant $\tfrac13$ is revealed
to be four times the quartic Taylor coefficient of the activation's remainder.

The moral is compact: cancellation between polarisation branches is a
monotonicity statement about the remainder, not a size statement about it.

\end{document}

